As a heavy Emacs user, I don't find these ideas
about Emacs doing things totally unrelated to
editing to be at all bizarre.  I use Emacs for a
number of things that it wouldn't be reasonable
to do in any normal text editor.  That's kind of
the whole point.  The reason Emacs is such a
great editor -- the reason it saves you so much
time -- is because you can make it do pretty
much anything.  It's not just a blank text box
into which you can type stuff.  It's a method
for streamlining your work by automating the
repetitive aspects of whatever you're doing
but leaving the parts interactive that need to
be.  Editing text files is perhaps the best
example anyone has ever come up with of
a task that tends to have highly repetitive
aspects that can benefit greatly from a
degree of automation but still requires very
significant amounts of human interaction
throughout the process.  Programming
source code is maybe the ultimate example.
Well, any software whose capabilities are
sufficiently general to do all that is ipso
facto going to be useful for things other
than editing files, as well.  Duh.

It's kind of like noticing that a well-equipped
kitchen can, if desired, be used for things
other than preparing food.

> Lots of programmers and managers today
> advocate things like Eclipse

I find it difficult to like anything that closely
associated with Java.  (I do like OO.o, but
that's _in spite of_ its Java association.)

> which chew up gigabytes (!) of memory,

Meh.  At the price of RAM these days, any
software that does what Emacs does would
be welcome to three or four gigs of it, as far
as I'm concerned.  The fact that Emacs
makes do with considerably less is mostly
a reflection of its advanced deferred loading
system, which was developed because it
was necessary in an earlier era when RAM
was much more scarce.

> need at least one high-resolution color display,

Is there any such thing as a workstation
that doesn't have that these days?  Heck,
a lot of *phones* have that now.  It hardly
seems like an egregious requirement.

> can't edit files very well,

That would be a problem.  Then again,
from the perspective of an Emacs user,
most editors can't edit files very well.
Most editors don't have even the most
basic features, like grouping-symbol
matching, sexp-based navigation,
regular expression search and replace,
automatic indentation, folding, or even
macros -- to say nothing of actual
*advanced* editing features.

